\title{40 percent of the machine work on Mathlib is the official way of comparing proofs, which the leaders skip}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P8.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: \src{docs/research/profiles/P8.md}; the 8-worker wall clock is from \src{docs/design/review/exact-proof-conversion.md}, section 6.2; [the profiling session](ses-JRSD). Per declaration, instructions inside the checks, joined by name over 714,712 units of Mathlib; native image \code{\verb~|-O3~}, one worker, machine with \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, in the arena container on the aarch64 development host. Counts are exact; the leaders were profiled per declaration with their arena builds.}
\ul{
\li{In instructions the machine is 6.5 times gruszka on Mathlib, 2,438.6 G against 373.8 G, and 4.7 times on Init; nano-lean needs 319.3 G.}
\li{The whole Mathlib-specific part is one class of work. 48 percent of the instructions of the machine are in 142,000 units, 20 percent of the units, in which it needs 8 to 200 times the instructions of gruszka. The other 80 percent of the units are at 4.1 times, the Init ratio.}
\li{Mechanism: two instance paths that meet are compared field by field, and every proof field and every proof argument goes through proof irrelevance, which compares the types of the two proofs, as the official kernel does. The statements of the laws are unfolded again on both sides.}
\li{gruszka and nano-lean never compare a proof argument or a proof field, and they try the arguments of two applications of one head before unfolding it.}
}
\transclude{ex-E344}
\table{
\tr{\th{Switches, all of Mathlib, every unit accepted}\th{Steps}\th{Instructions in the checks}\th{Times gruszka}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}}\td{1,511.5 M}\td{2,438.6 G}\td{6.52}}
\tr{\td{with \code{\verb~|facts~}, official-exact}\td{1,436.1 M}\td{2,410.5 G}\td{6.45}}
\tr{\td{the rich gated set, official-exact}\td{1,367.7 M}\td{2,212.5 G}\td{5.92}}
\tr{\td{with \code{\verb~|facts~} and \code{\verb~|skipargs~}, not official-exact}\td{876.8 M}\td{1,673.7 G}\td{4.48}}
\tr{\td{with \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} and \code{\verb~|congruence~}, not official-exact}\td{807.6 M}\td{1,640.5 G}\td{4.39}}
\tr{\td{with \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} and both}\td{680.1 M}\td{1,450.5 G, -40.5 percent}\td{3.88}}
}
\ul{
\li{The two options overlap: each alone removes about a third, because they answer the same comparisons from two sides.}
\li{On Init, Std and cedar the same options are worth 3.6, 5.0 and 6.0 percent: the mechanism is specific to the algebraic hierarchy and the category theory of Mathlib.}
\li{In the project metric: all of Mathlib with 8 workers, pipelined, warm, native image, on top of the larger default switch set, 25.39 s becomes 19.15 s, -24.6 percent of the wall clock.}
}
\p{So the answer to "why is Mathlib worse" is a policy, not a defect. The machine contains both shortcuts and kept them off because they are not the verdict of the official algorithm on hostile exports; they are [sound in the theory](ins-T1F1), and [no official-exact route recovers the cost](ins-FIXO). Under the practical policy of the maintainer they are admissible; see [standing policies](standing-policies).}
\p{See also: [the two proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts).}
